WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.779
David, how should we actually deal with police
brutality?

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:11.179
OK, in this segment, I'm not going to argue
about what percentage of cops are good or

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:17.470
bad or try to quantify the degree to which
police brutality and racialized policing is

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.470
a problem.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.880
It is a problem even.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:26.840
Listen, Rush Limbaugh said things that were
bought, you know, for Rush Limbaugh standards,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.920
conceding that we have a problem with policing
in this country.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.149
On The Breakfast Club earlier this week.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:33.809
Kellyanne Conway said it now.

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:36.559
Kellyanne Conway also said and Trump is dealing
with it.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:37.559
That's a lie.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:40.390
I'm not going to argue about the degree to
which it's a problem.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.809
It's a problem.

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:46.190
There are many issues with policing that need
to be solved.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.059
It doesn't matter if ninety two or ninety
four percent of police are good or whether

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:53.300
it's ninety nine point one versus ninety nine
point three percent.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.719
We've got a lot of issues that need to be
solved.

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:57.559
So here are some ideas from the point of.

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:00.670
So there's the legal side and there's the
behavior side.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.250
Let's talk first about how police behave.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.390
We need to be looking at training and a lot
of the infrastructure that's there.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.390
So.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.720
Police training in the United States should
be completely reworked for lack of a better

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.720
term.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:19.150
If you look at many other countries, officers
train much longer before they are just sent

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.700
out to be police.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.670
OK, now there are going to be arguments about
it costs too much.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.070
I'm telling you what should be done.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.430
Then we figure out how to make it happen.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.420
And by the way, we found six trillion bucks.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.409
I think we can find a way to better trained
police if we decide we want to do it.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:35.490
Germany, as an example, trains police much
longer.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:39.380
The United States, sometimes you've got like
a 12 or 13 week training program and then

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.710
you're out there.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.290
Sometimes it's, you know, six months or whatever.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.640
Police training needs to be longer.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.820
And a lot more of the training needs to be
more the type of training that social workers

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:51.820
get.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.869
Academic stuff based on the principles that
you would learn if you were studying for a

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:01.630
master in social work because you prevent
a lot of crime.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.140
What it straight out just you prevent having
to deal with crime.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.140
Then we'll get to how.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.140
How do you deal with crime?

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.050
You prevent a lot of crime.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.349
If you have police that are properly trained
as social workers, in addition, there's a

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:20.840
lot of stuff that's not actually criminal
or it's marginally criminalized as misdemeanors

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.950
that can or cannot be enforced based on the
discretion of officers, where if you have

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.849
officers better trained in de-escalation,
you have officers better trained with some

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:31.599
of these soft skills, as we might call them.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:34.370
You reduce the number of interactions that
become problematic.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:41.010
Number two, we need more extensive psychological
and personality testing of police, and we

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.220
want to just make sure we're bringing in the
right people.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.450
Again, most cops are good.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.769
Some, like Trump admitted on on Twitter, they're
looking for action.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:54.120
There's a sort of bloodlust in some fraction
of police officers.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.260
Those officers understand this.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.220
And I don't I don't want to get off topic
here.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:07.439
The bad apples, the bad eggs don't just typically
do one bad thing, they do a whole bunch.

00:03:07.439 --> 00:03:15.819
So a huge portion of the over overuse of force
complaints and other bad acts are concentrated

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:19.769
among a small number of repeat police offenders.

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:26.180
If we did a better job of evaluating and psych
testing and personality testing police, we

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.860
could root out a lot of the problem by never
hiring that small percentage that wants to

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.450
get out there and slap people around and is
looking to get the riot gear and do what we've

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.360
seen being done on videos.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.540
So root them out with it.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.909
More psychological and personality testing.

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:47.390
There should probably be some established
process for civilian review of shoot police

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.799
shootings and uses of force.

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:53.940
It can't just be left to police departments
to investigate because we all know that there

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.510
are many police chiefs that are doing this
properly.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:03.069
We know that there are internal internal affairs
boards that are operating more or less OK.

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:07.219
We should probably have more civilian involvement
in review of police shootings and uses of

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:08.219
force.

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:09.219
We've got to demilitarize.

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:12.130
The militarization of police forces has been
a huge problem.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.680
I've talked about that on its own, so I won't
delve too much into that.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.139
And there should probably be a national database
of bad police actors because it would prevent

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:22.139
two things.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:26.730
Number one, there's a national database of
of bad actors, people, police officers with

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.030
a high number of complaints against them.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:33.600
It would prevent officers from hiding prior
complaints by simply not reporting them when

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:34.600
they're trying to get hired.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.889
A new police department, because there would
be a national database of reporting to the

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:39.310
database, should be mandatory.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:44.070
But also, if you're hiring a police officer,
if you're not bringing them through police

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.940
academy and you're giving them their first
police job, it should be a requirement that

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.790
police departments have to check with that
central database so that the police department

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:56.479
can't get away with saying, we didn't know,
we just didn't know because it would be a

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.250
requirement to report complaints to the database
and to look at the database.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:07.210
If you're hiring someone now, then you've
got prosecution in terms of prosecution decisions

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:13.841
on prosecution of police officers and the
prosecution itself, meaning the trials, it

00:05:13.841 --> 00:05:19.350
should be happening ideally outside of the
county where the incidents took place.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:24.120
It can be very difficult to get what we would
all call a fair trial.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.470
Fair to the victims, fair to the officers,
fair to everybody, fair to the community when

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.949
a lot of these cases are being tried inside
the jurisdiction.

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:37.531
Now, by the jury, you might be outside of
the town, but still in the county and in a

00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:40.150
lot of America, that's not far enough.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.789
That's another thing that should should be
done.

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:47.600
You've got to try and prosecute these cases
outside, realistically, of the county where

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:48.780
they took place.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:49.780
Some ideas.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:50.780
There are many more.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:50.786
I'd love to hear from you.

